@page "/blocks/pages-as-data"
@using Stil.Docs.Examples.Blocks

<DocsHeader Title="Pages as data" Eyebrow="Website">
    <Lead>A page as JSON: a list of blocks with their settings, read from a database or a file and rendered with StilPage.</Lead>
</DocsHeader>

<p>
    The <a href="blocks/sections">page blocks</a> can be written in Razor, or kept as data, so a page can change
    without a new build: in a database, a file in the repository, or an editor of your own. The model is in the
    Stil package (<code>Stil.Blazor.Blocks</code>) and has no server parts, so it works in static server rendering,
    on the server and in WebAssembly. <code>dotnet new stil-site</code> keeps its pages this way, in SQLite.
</p>

<h2 id="example">A page from JSON</h2>
<DocExample Type="typeof(PageFromJson)" ShowCode>
    <p>
        <code>StilPageJson.Deserialize</code> reads a page and <code>&lt;StilPage&gt;</code> renders its blocks with
        the same components as in Razor, so the result is the same HTML. A page has a <code>path</code>, a
        <code>title</code>, a <code>description</code> and a <code>language</code> for your routing and the page's
        head, and its <code>blocks</code> in order. A block has a <code>type</code>, its <code>settings</code> and
        its <code>items</code>, such as the features of a feature grid or the buttons of a hero.
    </p>
</DocExample>

<h2 id="format">The format</h2>
<p>
    Names are in camelCase, a choice is in kebab case (<code>"tone": "muted"</code>), a date is
    <code>yyyy-MM-dd</code>, a picture is <code>{ "src", "alt" }</code>, and long text separates paragraphs with a
    blank line. Settings that are not set are left out. The file has a <code>version</code>: a page written by a
    newer version of Stil is refused with a <code>JsonException</code> rather than read wrongly. Comments and
    trailing commas are allowed, for files written by hand. The serialization is source-generated, so it also works
    in a trimmed WebAssembly app.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@Store" />

<h2 id="types">Block types</h2>
<p>
    <code>StilBlockCatalog.Default</code> describes the built-in types: each setting's name, label, kind (text,
    long text, yes/no, a choice, an icon, a picture, a link, a list or a date), whether it is required, and a line
    of help. An editor can be built from that, and the next part of Stil does. Every section block has an anchor
    (<code>id</code>), a background (<code>tone</code>), an eyebrow, a title, a lead and <code>centered</code>.
</p>
<div class="stil-table-responsive">
    <table class="stil-table stil-table-compact">
        <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Type</th><th scope="col">Block</th><th scope="col">Items</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            @foreach (var definition in StilBlockCatalog.Default.All)
            {
                <tr>
                    <td><code>@definition.Type</code></td>
                    <td>@definition.Title: @definition.Description</td>
                    <td>@(definition.Items is { } items ? $"{items.Title} ({items.Type})" : "")</td>
                </tr>
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>
<p>
    The <code>contact</code> block shows a contact form when it has a <code>formAction</code>: a plain form that
    posts <code>name</code>, <code>email</code> and <code>message</code>, with an antiforgery token, to an endpoint
    of your site. A type that is not in the catalog renders nothing, so an old page still renders when a type is
    removed.
</p>

<h2 id="your-own">Your own block types</h2>
<p>
    Add a type to a <code>StilBlockCatalog</code> with the component that renders it, and register the catalog as a
    service. The component gets the block as its <code>Data</code> parameter.
</p>
<CodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@Custom" />

<h2 id="api">API</h2>
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilPage)" />
<ApiTable Type="typeof(StilBlockView)" />

@code {
    private const string Store = """
        // Read a page, from wherever you keep it.
        StilPageData page = StilPageJson.Deserialize(await File.ReadAllTextAsync("pages/home.json"));

        // Or build one in code, and save it.
        var home = new StilPageData { Path = "/", Title = "Plan the week" }
            .Add(StilBlockData.Create("hero").Set("title", "Plan the week in ten minutes")
                .Add(StilBlockData.Create("action").Set("text", "Start free").Set("href", "signup")));
        await File.WriteAllTextAsync("pages/home.json", StilPageJson.Serialize(home));
        """;

    private const string Custom = """
        // Program.cs
        builder.Services.AddSingleton(new StilBlockCatalog()
            .Add(new StilBlockDefinition("quote", "Quote", [new("text", "Quote", StilBlockSettingKind.LongText)])
            {
                Component = typeof(QuoteBlock),
            }));

        // QuoteBlock.razor
        <blockquote>@Data.GetText("text")</blockquote>

        @code {
            [Parameter] public StilBlockData Data { get; set; } = new();
        }
        """;
}
